  "lessonBasics": "Meet the cube",
  "lessonBasicsDesc": "Pieces, faces and move names.",
  "stepBasicsPieces": "Three kinds of pieces",
  "stepBasicsPiecesBody": "Centers never move and give each face its color. Edges have two colors, corners have three. Solving means moving every edge and corner next to the centers that match.",
  "stepBasicsFaces": "Six face letters",
  "stepBasicsFacesBody": "R, L, U, D, F and B are the right, left, up, down, front and back faces. A letter alone means a clockwise quarter turn, as if you looked straight at that face.",
  "stepBasicsPrime": "Prime and double turns",
  "stepBasicsPrimeBody": "An apostrophe (R') turns the other way, counter-clockwise. A 2 (U2) is a half turn: two quarter turns.",
  "stepCrossGoal": "The goal",
  "stepCrossGoalBody": "Hold yellow on top and green facing you. First gather the four white edges around the yellow center, white facing up, like petals: that's the daisy. Then each petal goes down to the white center, its side color matching the center beside it.",
  "stepCrossTop": "Edge on top",
  "stepCrossTopBody": "A white edge on top with white facing up is a daisy petal. Turn the top until its side color sits right above the center of the same color, then turn that face twice (180°) to drop it onto the white center.",
  "stepCrossMiddle": "Edge in the middle",
  "stepCrossMiddleBody": "An edge stuck in the middle layer goes up first: turn its side up, move the top out of the way and turn the side back, so placed pieces stay put. Now it's a petal on top: line it up and turn that face twice.",
  "stepCornersInsert": "Above its spot",
  "stepCornersInsertBody": "Turn the top until the white corner sits right above its slot, between the two centers that match its side colors. Here one top turn and R U' R' are enough: U brings the corner over its slot and R U' R' tucks it in, white facing down.",
  "stepCornersRepeat": "Repeat the trigger",
  "stepCornersRepeatBody": "Hold the slot at the front right and repeat R U R' U' until white faces down: once if white faces right, three times if it faces up, five times if it faces you. The top looks messy meanwhile; it sorts itself out.",
  "stepMiddleRight": "Edge to the right",
  "stepMiddleRightBody": "Find a top edge with no yellow on it. Turn the top until its front color matches the center facing you. If its top color matches the right center, send it right: U R U' R' U' F' U F.",
  "stepMiddleLeft": "Edge to the left",
  "stepMiddleLeftBody": "If its top color matches the left center instead, use the mirror: U' L' U L U F U' F'. The white layer comes back whole every time.",
  "stepYellowCross": "One algorithm",
  "stepYellowCrossBody": "Look only at the yellow edges on top. Hold an L at the back left and a line from left to right, then do F R U R' U' F'. A line needs it once, an L twice, a dot up to three times.",
  "stepYellowEdges": "Match the edges",
  "stepYellowEdgesBody": "Turn the top until two edges match their side centers. With one at the back and one on the right, R U R' U R U2 R' U swaps the front and left edges.",
  "stepPlaceCorners": "Place them",
  "stepPlaceCornersBody": "A corner is home when its colors match the three centers around it, even if it's twisted. Hold a home corner at the front right: U R U' L' U R' U' L moves the other three around. Repeat until all four are home.",
  "stepTwistCorners": "Twist them",
  "stepTwistCornersBody": "With an unsolved corner at the front right, repeat R' D' R D in pairs until yellow is on top, then turn only the top to bring the next one. The bottom looks broken until the very last corner: keep going.",
  "lessonCfopIntro": "What is CFOP?",
  "lessonCfopIntroDesc": "Cross, F2L, OLL, PLL.",
  "stepCfopOverview": "Four stages",
  "stepCfopOverviewBody": "Speedcubers solve the cross, then pair each first-layer corner with its middle edge (F2L), orient the last layer (OLL) and finally permute it (PLL).",
  "stepCfopCross": "Plan the cross",
  "stepCfopCrossBody": "In CFOP the cross is planned during inspection and built on the bottom in about eight moves.",
  "lessonF2l": "F2L basics",
  "lessonF2lDesc": "Pair a corner with its edge.",
  "stepF2lRight": "Insert a pair",
  "stepF2lRightBody": "When a corner and its edge sit side by side on top, bring them above their slot and insert them together.",
  "stepF2lLeft": "Left-hand pair",
  "stepF2lLeftBody": "The same case on the other side is the mirror, U' L' U L. Rotating the cube (y) lets you use whichever hand is easier.",
  "lessonOll": "Two-look OLL",
  "lessonOllDesc": "Make the top yellow.",
  "stepOllCross": "Edges first",
  "stepOllCrossBody": "Two-look OLL starts with the yellow-cross algorithm you already know.",
  "stepOllSune": "Sune",
  "stepOllSuneBody": "With one yellow corner facing up at the front left, Sune (R U R' U R U2 R') orients the other three. Most other cases use Sune and its mirror.",
  "lessonPll": "Two-look PLL",
  "lessonPllDesc": "Finish the cube.",
  "stepPllT": "T-perm",
  "stepPllTBody": "The T-perm swaps two corners and two edges. Use it to place the corners: hold the two corners that must swap on the right.",
  "stepPllU": "U-perm",
  "stepPllUBody": "With the corners done, the U-perm cycles three edges. Keep the finished side at the back.",
  "lessonTwoFirst": "2×2 first layer",
  "lessonTwoFirstDesc": "Build one side with matching corners.",
  "stepTwoIntro": "Only corners",
  "stepTwoIntroBody": "A 2×2 is only eight corners: no edges, no centers. Build the white layer on the bottom so neighbouring side colors match, then finish on one route: Sune for a yellow top, then the T-perm or Y-perm.",
  "stepTwoCorner": "Insert corners",
  "stepTwoCornerBody": "Like the 3×3 white corners: bring a corner above its empty slot at the front right and tuck it in, with U R U' R' here or by repeating R U R' U' until white faces down. Check its side colors against its neighbour.",
  "lessonTwoOll": "Orient the top",
  "lessonTwoOllDesc": "Ortega OLL with Sune.",
  "stepTwoSune": "Sune again",
  "stepTwoSuneBody": "Count the yellow stickers facing up. Hold the one yellow-up corner at the front left and do Sune (R U R' U R U2 R'). Count again and re-hold; it takes three Sunes at most. Turn only the top between them.",
  "lessonTwoPbl": "Permute both layers",
  "lessonTwoPblDesc": "Swap corners to finish.",
  "stepTwoAdjacent": "Adjacent swap",
  "stepTwoAdjacentBody": "Headlights are two matching top stickers on one side. Turn the top until the headlights are on the left, so the two corners to swap are on the right, then do the T-perm.",
  "stepTwoDiagonal": "Diagonal swap",
  "stepTwoDiagonalBody": "No headlights anywhere means two diagonal corners must swap: do the Y-perm from any side, then turn the top to line everything up.",
  "lessonBigIntro": "Big cube pieces",
  "lessonBigIntroDesc": "Centers, wings and slices.",
  "stepBigPieces": "No fixed centers",
  "stepBigPiecesBody": "On a 4×4 each face has four center pieces that move around, and every edge is two wings. No center is fixed, so check the color order on a corner: white opposite yellow, blue opposite green, red opposite orange.",
  "stepBigSlices": "Wide and inner turns",
  "stepBigSlicesBody": "Rw turns the two right layers together. 2R turns only the second layer from the right. These turns move centers and wings.",
  "lessonBigCenters": "Centers",
  "lessonBigCentersDesc": "Group the center pieces.",
  "stepBigCenterCycle": "A center 3-cycle",
  "stepBigCenterCycleBody": "This commutator moves exactly three center pieces and leaves everything else alone, so finished centers stay finished. Use it when one stray piece won't join a bar.",
  "lessonBigEdges": "Pair the edges",
  "lessonBigEdgesDesc": "Join wings into full edges.",
  "stepBigEdgePair": "Pair and restore",
  "stepBigEdgePairBody": "Uw' lines up two matching wings, R U R' F R' F' R swaps in an unpaired edge, and Uw brings the centers back. Always close a slice you opened.",
  "lessonBigParity": "Parity",
  "lessonBigParityDesc": "OLL and PLL parity on even cubes.",
  "stepBigOllParity": "OLL parity",
  "stepBigOllParityBody": "A single flipped edge can't happen on a 3×3: it's OLL parity, not a mistake. Keep it at the front of the top layer and play the long algorithm. It also swaps the left and right top edges and two corners; the last 3×3 steps fix those.",
  "stepBigPllParity": "PLL parity",
  "stepBigPllParityBody": "Two swapped edges at the very end, and no 3×3 algorithm fits? That's PLL parity. Hold them at the front and back of the top layer: 2R2 U2 2R2 Uw2 2R2 2U2 swaps them.",
  "introGoalHeading": "What's the goal?",
  "introHowHeading": "How do we do it?",
  "introHoldHeading": "How to hold it",
  "introWhyHeading": "Why it works",
  "introStart": "Got it, start step by step",
  "introDontShowAgain": "Don't show automatically again",
  "@introDontShowAgain": {
    "description": "Switch in the sub-step goal sheet: stop opening it automatically at each new sub-step (the flag button still opens it)."
  },
  "introShowGoal": "Show the goal",
  "introStateStart": "Start",
  "introStateGoal": "Goal",
  "introPlayMorph": "Play from start to goal",
  "introGoalBasics": "Learn to read the cube: its pieces, its six faces and how the move letters turn them.",
  "introGoalCross": "Make a white plus sign on the bottom where every white edge also matches the center color beside it.",
  "introGoalCorners": "Fill in the four white corners so the whole first layer is done: a white face with matching side colors.",
  "introGoalMiddle": "Place the four middle-layer edges so the first two layers are solved.",
  "introGoalYellowCross": "Make a yellow plus sign on top; the side colors don't need to match yet.",
  "introGoalYellowEdges": "Line up every yellow cross edge with the center color on its side.",
  "introGoalYellowCorners": "Move every yellow corner to its own spot, even if it is still twisted.",
  "introGoalTwistCorners": "Twist the yellow corners the right way so the whole cube is solved.",
  "introGoalCfopIntro": "See the speedcubing plan: cross, first two layers, then the last layer in two looks.",
  "introGoalF2l": "Insert each white corner together with its middle edge, finishing two layers at once.",
  "introGoalOll": "Make the whole top face yellow with one or two algorithms.",
  "introGoalPll": "Move the last-layer pieces to their spots to finish the cube.",
  "introGoalTwoFirst": "Build one complete white layer on the 2×2.",
  "introGoalTwoOll": "Make the top face of the 2×2 all yellow.",
  "introGoalTwoPbl": "Put the last corners home with one T-perm or Y-perm, then line up the top to solve the 2×2.",
  "introGoalBigIntro": "Meet the 4×4: centers, edge pairs and corners, and the moves that turn them.",
  "introGoalBigCenters": "Gather each face's center pieces into one solid block of color.",
  "introGoalBigEdges": "Pair up matching edge pieces so every edge looks like a single 3×3 edge.",
  "introGoalBigParity": "Fix the two 4×4 cases a 3×3 never has: one flipped edge (OLL parity) or two swapped edges (PLL parity).",
  "introHowBasics": "Centers never move, so they tell you each face's color.\nEach letter turns one face a quarter turn clockwise.\nAn apostrophe (') turns it back; a 2 turns it twice.",
  "introHoldBasics": "Any way you like: the centers stay in place, so the colors always tell you which face is which.",
  "introWhyBasics": "Every algorithm is just these letters in a row, so reading them is the first skill.",
  "introHowCross": "Bring each white edge to the top, white facing up, around the yellow center: that's the daisy.\nTurn the top until a petal's side color sits above its matching center.\nTurn that face twice to drop it down; repeat for all four.",
  "introHoldCross": "Yellow center on top, white center on the bottom, for the whole step. Turn the top layer, not the cube, to line up a petal.",
  "introWhyCross": "With white facing up on top, two turns of the front face land the edge on the white center, white facing down, already matching its side.",
  "introHowCorners": "Turn the top until a white corner sits above its empty slot, and hold that slot at the front right.\nRepeat R U R' U': once if white faces right, three times if it faces up, five if it faces you.\nDo the same for the other three corners.",
  "introHoldCorners": "White on the bottom, yellow on top. The corner sits at the front right, directly above its slot.",
  "introWhyCorners": "R U R' U' lifts the slot and puts it back: each round twists the corner a little until white faces down, and the cross is never broken.",
  "introHowMiddle": "Find a top edge with no yellow on it and match its front color to the center below.\nIts top color says where it goes: right or left.\nSend it with the 8-move insert; the white layer comes back every time.",
  "introHoldMiddle": "White on the bottom, yellow on top. The edge sits on the front, its front color matching the center below it.",
  "introWhyMiddle": "The insert lifts the slot out of the way, slides the edge in and brings the white layer back. An edge with yellow belongs on top, never in the middle.",
  "introHowYellowCross": "Look only at the yellow edges on top: a dot, an L or a line.\nHold the L at the back left, the line from left to right, and do F R U R' U' F'.\nA line needs it once, an L twice, a dot up to three times.",
  "introHoldYellowCross": "White on the bottom, yellow on top. An L at the back left, a line from left to right; a dot any way.",
  "introWhyYellowCross": "F R U R' U' F' flips two top edges up at a time without touching the first two layers.",
  "introHowYellowEdges": "Turn the top until two edges match their side centers.\nHold them at the back and on the right, then do R U R' U R U2 R' U.\nIf the matched edges are opposite, do it once from any side first.",
  "introHoldYellowEdges": "Yellow on top. One matched edge at the back and the other on the right.",
  "introWhyYellowEdges": "This algorithm only moves top pieces, so the first two layers always come back.",
  "introHowYellowCorners": "A corner is home when its colors match the three centers around it, even if twisted.\nKeep a home corner at the front right.\nU R U' L' U R' U' L cycles the other three; repeat until all four fit.",
  "introHoldYellowCorners": "Yellow on top. A corner that is already home at the front right; if none is, any corner.",
  "introWhyYellowCorners": "It moves three corners in a circle and leaves the one at the front right in place, so every repeat brings more corners home.",
  "introHowTwistCorners": "Hold an unsolved corner at the front right.\nRepeat R' D' R D in pairs until its yellow faces up.\nTurn only the top for the next one; the bottom comes back with the last corner.",
  "introHoldTwistCorners": "Yellow on top, the corner to twist at the front right. Between corners turn only the top, never the whole cube.",
  "introWhyTwistCorners": "R' D' R D twists the corner and scrambles the bottom, but the bottom returns once every corner is twisted. Stopping halfway leaves it broken.",
  "introHowCfopIntro": "Cross: four edges, planned during inspection.\nF2L: each corner goes in together with its edge.\nOLL makes the top yellow, then PLL moves the pieces home.",
  "introHoldCfopIntro": "Speedcubers build the cross on the bottom, so they can look ahead at the next pieces while they turn.",
  "introWhyCfopIntro": "Solving a corner and its edge together saves many moves, and recognizing cases replaces trial and error.",
  "introHowF2l": "Find a corner and its matching edge in the top layer.\nPair them up above their slot.\nDrop the pair in with three or four moves.",
  "introHoldF2l": "Cross on the bottom. The pair's slot at the front right, or the front left for the mirror.",
  "introWhyF2l": "A paired corner and edge drop in together, so two layers grow at once instead of one piece at a time.",
  "introHowOll": "First the yellow cross with F R U R' U' F'.\nThen orient the corners, for example with Sune.\nRecognize the case by the yellow you see.",
  "introHoldOll": "Yellow on top. For Sune, the one corner with yellow facing up goes at the front left.",
  "introWhyOll": "Splitting the last layer into orient first, then permute keeps the number of algorithms small.",
  "introHowPll": "The top is already all yellow.\nThe T-perm swaps corners; the U-perm cycles three edges.\nRead the side colors to choose the algorithm.",
  "introHoldPll": "Yellow on top. T-perm: headlights on the left. U-perm: the finished side at the back.",
  "introWhyPll": "Once the top is all yellow, only the pieces' places are wrong, and a few swaps finish the cube.",
  "introHowTwoFirst": "Pick a white corner as your anchor, white facing down.\nBring each next corner above its slot at the front right.\nRepeat R U R' U' until it drops in with matching side colors.",
  "introHoldTwoFirst": "White on the bottom. The empty slot at the front right, its corner right above it on the top layer.",
  "introWhyTwoFirst": "A 2×2 has no centers: the first corner you place sets the colors of its neighbours, so side colors matter, not only white.",
  "introHowTwoOll": "Count the corners with yellow facing up.\nWith one up, hold it at the front left and do Sune: R U R' U R U2 R'.\nCount again and repeat; three Sunes at most.",
  "introHoldTwoOll": "White on the bottom, yellow on top. The corner with yellow up at the front left before each Sune.",
  "introWhyTwoOll": "Sune twists the top corners without breaking the white layer; recounting each time tells you how to hold it next.",
  "introHowTwoPbl": "Look for headlights: two matching top stickers on one side.\nHeadlights on one side: hold them on the left and do the T-perm.\nNo headlights: do the Y-perm from any side, then line up the top.",
  "introHoldTwoPbl": "White on the bottom, yellow on top. T-perm: headlights on the left. Y-perm: any side facing you.",
  "introWhyTwoPbl": "With the top yellow, only the corners' places are wrong: the T-perm swaps two neighbours, the Y-perm two diagonals.",
  "introHowBigIntro": "No center is fixed: four center pieces make up each face color.\nWide moves like Rw turn two layers at once.\nInner moves like 2R turn just one inner slice.",
  "introHoldBigIntro": "Any way you like at first: on a 4×4 no center is fixed, so you choose where each color goes.",
  "introWhyBigIntro": "Reduction groups centers and edge pieces so the big cube can be solved like a 3×3.",
  "introHowBigCenters": "Check the color order from a corner first.\nBuild two opposite centers, then the other four.\nInner slices move center pieces; always undo a slice you opened.",
  "introHoldBigCenters": "The face you are building in front, finished centers out of the way on the left and right.",
  "introWhyBigCenters": "A slice moves centers, and undoing it restores the finished ones, so only the piece you aimed for changes.",
  "introHowBigEdges": "Find the two wings with the same two colors.\nLine them up on the front and join them with Uw'.\nSwap in an unpaired edge, then Uw brings the centers back.",
  "introHoldBigEdges": "The edge you are pairing in the front layers; finished edges parked in the top and bottom layers.",
  "introWhyBigEdges": "Uw' moves the centers aside so two wings can meet; Uw puts the centers back, keeping your work.",
  "introHowBigParity": "Solve the 4×4 like a 3×3 until it gets stuck.\nOne flipped edge: the long OLL parity algorithm.\nTwo swapped edges: 2R2 U2 2R2 Uw2 2R2 2U2.",
  "introHoldBigParity": "The last layer on top. OLL parity: the flipped edge at the front. PLL parity: the swapped edges at the front and back.",
  "introWhyBigParity": "Edge wings can end up in states a 3×3 can't reach; these algorithms turn inner layers to undo exactly that.",
