  "solveAllStagesDone": "Every stage complete. Well done!",
  "trackFiveByFive": "5×5 Professor's Cube",
  "trackSixBySix": "6×6 cube",
  "lessonFiveIntro": "Meet the 5×5",
  "lessonFiveIntroDesc": "Fixed middles, center bars, middle edges and wings.",
  "introGoalFiveIntro": "Get to know the 5×5: fixed middles, center bars, middle edges and wings.",
  "introHowFiveIntro": "The very middle of each face never moves.\nEach edge is a middle piece with two wings.\nInner slices move pieces without touching the outside.",
  "introHoldFiveIntro": "Any way you like: on a 5×5 the very middle of each face never moves, so it shows that face's color.",
  "introWhyFiveIntro": "Fixed middles set the color scheme, so the centers can't be built in the wrong order.",
  "lessonFiveCenters": "5×5 centers",
  "lessonFiveCentersDesc": "Build each center from bars of three.",
  "introGoalFiveCenters": "Build every face's 3×3 block of center pieces in one color.",
  "introHowFiveCenters": "Start from the fixed middle piece: it sets the color.\nBuild bars of three and slide them in with an inner slice.\nThe move that brings a bar in also restores the finished center.",
  "introHoldFiveCenters": "The center you are building in front or on top; finished centers on the bottom, left or right, out of the way.",
  "introWhyFiveCenters": "Slice in, turn the top, slice back: the slice that brings the bar in also restores the finished centers.",
  "lessonFiveEdges": "5×5 edges",
  "lessonFiveEdgesDesc": "Join two wings to their middle edge.",
  "introGoalFiveEdges": "Join each middle edge with its two wings so all 12 edges look like single pieces.",
  "introHowFiveEdges": "Match each wing to its middle edge on the front.\nTurn the inner layer, swap in an unsolved edge, turn it back.\nFor the last edges, use slice, flip, slice.",
  "introHoldFiveEdges": "The edge you are building at the front; finished edges stored in the top and bottom layers.",
  "introWhyFiveEdges": "Opening and closing the inner slice joins wings while centers and stored edges come back.",
  "lessonFiveSolve": "Solve the 5×5 as a 3×3",
  "lessonFiveSolveDesc": "Finish layer by layer, like a 3×3.",
  "introGoalFiveSolve": "Solve the reduced 5×5 like a 3×3, layer by layer, with outer turns only.",
  "introHowFiveSolve": "Only outer turns from now on, or paired edges split apart.\nWhite cross and corners, the middle layer, then the yellow layer.\nThe fixed middles show each face's color.",
  "introHoldFiveSolve": "The first layer's color on the bottom, exactly as on a 3×3; the corner to insert at the front right.",
  "introWhyFiveSolve": "Inner turns would split centers and edges again; outer turns treat each block as one 3×3 piece.",
  "lessonFiveParity": "5×5 edge parity",
  "lessonFiveParityDesc": "Fix the edge whose wings are swapped.",
  "introGoalFiveParity": "Fix the one edge whose two outer wings are swapped: the only parity a 5×5 has.",
  "introHowFiveParity": "It shows up as one last edge that looks flipped.\nPlay the long inner-slice fix once.\nThere is no PLL parity on a 5×5.",
  "introHoldFiveParity": "The last layer on top, the edge with swapped wings at the front of the top layer.",
  "introWhyFiveParity": "Only the two outer wings of one edge can swap on a 5×5, so a single algorithm covers it.",
  "lessonSixIntro": "Meet the 6×6",
  "lessonSixIntroDesc": "No fixed centers and four pieces in every edge.",
  "introGoalSixIntro": "Get to know the 6×6: sixteen center pieces per face and four pieces per edge.",
  "introHowSixIntro": "No center is fixed: you choose the colors as you build.\nEach edge has outer and inner wings.\nWide turns can move up to three layers.",
  "introHoldSixIntro": "Any way you like at first: like a 4×4, a 6×6 has no fixed center.",
  "introWhySixIntro": "If you can solve a 4×4 and a 5×5, the 6×6 is the same reduction with bigger blocks.",
  "lessonSixCenters": "6×6 centers",
  "lessonSixCentersDesc": "Build 4×4 centers from bars of four.",
  "introGoalSixCenters": "Build every face's 4×4 center block in one solid color.",
  "introHowSixCenters": "Check the color order from a corner first: no center is fixed.\nBuild bars of four and slide them together.\nFor the last two centers, move single pieces with slice, top, undo.",
  "introHoldSixCenters": "The face you are building in front, finished centers on the left, right or bottom.",
  "introWhySixCenters": "Bars join with inner slices, and every slice is undone, so finished centers stay whole.",
  "lessonSixEdges": "6×6 edges",
  "lessonSixEdgesDesc": "Gather the four pieces of every edge.",
  "introGoalSixEdges": "Gather the four pieces of each edge so the 6×6 has 12 clean edges.",
  "introHowSixEdges": "Line up the outer and inner wings of one edge on the front.\nThe deep wide layer brings an edge in and out.\nNever break a finished edge to free a piece.",
  "introHoldSixEdges": "The edge you are building at the front; finished edges parked in the top and bottom layers.",
  "introWhySixEdges": "The deep wide turn moves centers aside so wings can meet, then brings them back.",
  "lessonSixSolve": "Solve the 6×6 as a 3×3",
  "lessonSixSolveDesc": "Finish layer by layer, like a 3×3.",
  "introGoalSixSolve": "Solve the reduced 6×6 like a 3×3, layer by layer, with outer turns only.",
  "introHowSixSolve": "Only outer turns from now on: a slice would split a center or an edge.\nThe centers you built show each face's color.\nWhite cross and corners, the middle layer, then the yellow layer.",
  "introHoldSixSolve": "The first layer's color on the bottom, exactly as on a 3×3; the corner to insert at the front right.",
  "introWhySixSolve": "Outer turns move each 4×4 center and each edge as one piece, just like on a 3×3.",
  "lessonSixParity": "6×6 parity",
  "lessonSixParityDesc": "One flipped edge or two swapped edges.",
  "introGoalSixParity": "Fix the two 6×6 cases a 3×3 never has: one flipped edge or two swapped edges.",
  "introHowSixParity": "Solve like a 3×3 until it gets stuck.\nOne flipped edge: the long parity fix with 3Rw.\nTwo swapped edges: the short fix of inner double turns.",
  "introHoldSixParity": "The last layer on top. The flipped edge at the front; for two swapped edges, the front and back.",
  "introWhySixParity": "Like a 4×4, a 6×6 can end with one flipped or two swapped edges; inner-layer algorithms fix them and leave the rest alone.",
  "stepFivePieces": "The pieces of a 5×5",
  "stepFivePiecesBody": "The very middle piece of each face never moves, so the color scheme can't come out wrong. Around it sit eight center pieces: corner centers and edge centers never swap types. Each edge is a middle piece with two wings.",
  "stepFiveSlices": "Three kinds of turns",
  "stepFiveSlicesBody": "Outer turns move one face. Wide turns move a face with the layer behind it. Inner slice turns move only a layer inside: that is how pieces change place without touching the outside.",
  "stepFiveCenterBar": "Center bars",
  "stepFiveCenterBarBody": "Build a row of three matching center pieces, slide it in with an inner slice, turn the top and slide back: the move that brings the bar in also restores the finished center. Three bars make a center.",
  "stepFiveCenterSwap": "Swap without breaking",
  "stepFiveCenterSwapBody": "To move a single center piece: turn an inner slice, turn the top, undo the slice, turn the top back. Finished centers are restored every time.",
  "stepFiveEdgePair": "Pair wings with a middle edge",
  "stepFiveEdgePairBody": "Line up a wing beside its matching middle edge on the front, turn the inner layer down, swap in an unsolved edge with a short trigger, then turn the inner layer back.",
  "stepFiveEdgeLast": "The last edges",
  "stepFiveEdgeLastBody": "With no free edge left to swap in, use slice, flip, slice: a wide turn, a short trigger and the wide turn back pair the last edges without breaking finished ones.",
  "stepFiveAsThree": "Now it is a 3×3",
  "stepFiveAsThreeBody": "With centers and edges done, the 5×5 behaves like a big 3×3. Use only outer turns and go layer by layer: white cross and corners, the middle layer, then the yellow layer. A 5×5 has no PLL parity.",
  "stepFiveEdgeParity": "Swap the last edge's wings",
  "stepFiveEdgeParityBody": "Sometimes the last edge has its two outer wings swapped, so it looks flipped: the 5×5's only parity. Hold it at the front of the top layer and play the inner-slice algorithm once; everything else returns to its place.",
  "stepSixPieces": "The pieces of a 6×6",
  "stepSixPiecesBody": "Like a 4×4, a 6×6 has no fixed center: sixteen center pieces make up each face color, so check the color order on a corner first. Each edge has four wings: two outer and two inner.",
  "stepSixSlices": "Turning deeper layers",
  "stepSixSlicesBody": "Wide turns can move two or three layers from a face. Inner slices on the second or third layer are the tools for centers and edges.",
  "stepSixCenterBar": "Bars of four",
  "stepSixCenterBarBody": "Build rows of four matching center pieces and slide them next to each other with inner slice turns, undoing each slice, until the face is one solid 4×4 block.",
  "stepSixCenterSwap": "Move one piece at a time",
  "stepSixCenterSwapBody": "Turn the third layer, turn the top, undo, turn back: one center piece moves while finished centers come back. Use it for the last two centers, when no bar fits.",
  "stepSixEdgePair": "Four pieces, one edge",
  "stepSixEdgePairBody": "Collect the four wings of an edge on the front with the deep wide layer, swap in an unsolved edge with a short trigger, then turn the wide layer back.",
  "stepSixEdgeLast": "The last edges",
  "stepSixEdgeLastBody": "Pair the final edges with a short trigger between a deep wide turn and its undo, so finished edges stay paired. Never break a finished edge to free a piece.",
  "stepSixAsThree": "Now it is a 3×3",
  "stepSixAsThreeBody": "With 4×4 centers and paired edges, solve the 6×6 like a 3×3, layer by layer, with outer turns only. One flipped or two swapped edges at the end is parity, not a mistake.",
  "stepSixOllParity": "One flipped edge",
  "stepSixOllParityBody": "A single edge that looks flipped in the last layer is parity. Hold it at the front of the top layer and play the long fix: it flips all four wings, also swaps the left and right top edges, and leaves the first two layers untouched.",
  "stepSixPllParity": "Two swapped edges",
  "stepSixPllParityBody": "If two edges look swapped at the very end, hold them at the front and back of the top layer and play the short fix of inner double turns: all four wings of both edges trade places.",
  "stepSixWingRule": "Four wings or two?",
  "@stepSixWingRule": {
    "description": "6x6 parity lesson: title of the rule telling the two flipped-edge cases apart."
  },
  "stepSixWingRuleBody": "A 6×6 edge is made of 4 wings. Before fixing an edge that looks flipped, look closely: if all 4 wings are flipped, use the standard 3-layer OLL parity; if only the 2 inner wings are flipped, use inner-wing parity. The cube shows the inner-wing case.",
  "@stepSixWingRuleBody": {
    "description": "6x6 parity lesson: all 4 wings flipped takes 3-layer OLL parity, only the 2 inner wings flipped takes inner-wing parity."
  },
  "stepSixInnerWingParity": "Two inner wings flipped",
  "@stepSixInnerWingParity": {
    "description": "6x6 parity lesson: title of the inner-wing parity example."
  },
  "stepSixInnerWingParityBody": "Here the outer wings of the front top edge are fine and only the 2 inner wings are flipped. Keep that edge at the front of the top layer and turn only the third slices (3R, 3L): the inner wings flip back and the rest of the cube stays solved.",
  "@stepSixInnerWingParityBody": {
    "description": "6x6 parity lesson: explanation of the inner-wing parity example (3R / 3L slices only)."
  },
  "stageTwoFirstLayer": "Build the bottom layer",
  "stageTwoTopColor": "Make the top one color",
  "stageTwoFinish": "Finish the corners",
  "explainTwoFirstLayer": "Complete the bottom layer: its four corners match each other and the side colors.",
  "explainTwoTopColor": "Turn the top until all four top stickers show the same color, keeping the bottom layer.",
  "explainTwoFinish": "Move the last corners into place to finish the cube.",
